  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nIn the extended portion of the forecast, the latest data continues \nto show the upper level pattern deamplifying a bit as we head into \nthe late weekend and into early next week. The upper trough axis \nfrom the weekend is expected to shift eastward while a more zonal \nflow aloft develops across the CONUS.  The main belt of westerlies \nwill shift back to the north with the main MCS track running from \nthe southern High Plains eastward into the upper Midwest and into \nthe Great Lakes.  Locally, we'll see temperatures ramp back up into \nthe lower 90s for the Sunday through Tuesday period with mainly dry \nconditions on Sunday and Monday.  Tuesday afternoon we could see \nsome diurnally driven pulse convection develop across the area. \nHowever, overall coverage looks to be pretty isolated at best.\n\nBy mid-late week, the pattern is forecast to amplify with ridging \ndeveloping out across the western US and downstream troughing \ndeveloping across the east.  This will set up the Ohio Valley into a \nnorthwest flow regime with embedded perturbations pushing through \nwithin the mean flow aloft.  The net result here will be episodic \nbouts of convection (possible MCSs) moving through the region.  Some \nof these bouts of convection may produce strong/severe storms. \nRepeated bouts of convection training across the region could result \nin excessive rainfall and hydro issues as we move through mid-late \nweek time frame.  Temperatures will likely be a bit cooler than in \nthe earlier part of the week, given the northwest flow and expected \nbouts of convection.  Daytime highs will generally be in the middle \n80s with overnight lows in the upper 60s to the lower 70s.",
